0	102	The Juncker Commission was the European Commission in office from 1 November 2014 to 30 November 2019.
103	287	Its president was Jean-Claude Juncker, who presided over 27 other commissioners (one from each of the states composing the European Union, except Luxembourg, which is Juncker's state).
288	421	In July 2014, Juncker was officially elected to succeed José Manuel Barroso, who completed his second five-year term in that year.



421	587	Election

In the 2014 parliamentary election, Juncker campaigned as the candidate  of the European People's Party (EPP) for the presidency of the European Commission.
588	691	The EPP won a plurality in parliament, and on 27 June, the European Council nominated him for the post.
692	787	Later on 15 July 2014, the European Parliament elected Juncker as the new Commission president.
788	991	On 22 October, the European Parliament approved the Juncker Commission in its entirety and during the 23–24 October 2014 meeting of the European Council the Council formally appointed the new Commissi
992	1057	On 1 November 2014, the new Commission officially assumed office.
1058	1148	Juncker has outlined a ten-point agenda for his Presidency focusing on jobs and growth.



1148	1238	Policy
Under the Juncker Commission, the EU General Data Protection Regulation was passed.
1239	1379	The Commission co-developed the law, culminating in a trilogue proposal between the Commission, Parliament, and Council on 15 December 2015.
1380	1424	The GDPR entered into force on 24 May 2016.

1424	1558	The Commission abolished retail telephone and mobile data roaming charges in the member states and some others (Roam Like At Home).



1558	1689	Institutional reorganisation
Juncker made the Commission's work more top-down by strengthening the Secretariat-General in two ways.
1690	1758	Firstly, he gave it a gatekeeper function regarding new initiatives.
1759	1929	All "major initiatives" must henceforth be approved by the "relevant Vice-President(s) and the First Vice-President, unless they are initiated directly by the President".
1930	2528	Secondly, the SG was made chair of all inter-service steering groups (ISGs) dealing with priority initiatives in the Commission's work programme.:9-10 :8-9
Juncker also abolished the position of Commi
2528	2539	None (1)



2539	2738	Former members


President's cabinet
The President's cabinet supports the President of the commission, and thus has a very central role in coordinating the work of the European Commission as a whole.
2739	2797	The president's cabinet is led by Clara Martinez Alberola.
2798	2906	Formerly, it was led by Martin Selmayr, who has been described as "the most powerful EU chief of staff ever.
2906	3046	"


Project teams
Juncker has for the first time proposed a commission that clusters certain members together under designated policy areas.
3047	3145	These clusters are known as "Project Teams" and will each be headed by one of the vice presidents.
3146	3265	Each team is composed of a core membership in addition to members who may fall under its respective umbrella as needed.
3266	3541	Timmermans and Georgieva both oversee all commissioners while the remaining five project teams are as follows:


